\section{Train/test separation and the grokking ratio}\label{sec:sep}

\begin{definition}
Let $E(p,t) = -1+\ReLU(tp)$, the width-one one-dimensional network with hidden weight
$1$, hidden bias $0$, output weight $1$ and output bias $-1$. Take training points with
signals $2$ (positive class) and $-1$ (negative class), and a test point with weak
signal $1/2$. The training set is \emph{perfect} at $t$ if $E(2,t)>0$ and
$-E(-1,t)>0$; the test point is \emph{correct} if $E(1/2,t)>0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact grokking window]
$\{\,t\ge0 :\ \text{training perfect and test incorrect}\,\} = \bigl(\tfrac12, 2\bigr]$.
Moreover the window is sharp on both sides: the training set is not perfectly
classified for $0\le t\le\tfrac12$, and the test point is correct for every $t>2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$E(2,t)=-1+\max(2t,0)>0$ iff $t>\tfrac12$; the negative point satisfies
$-E(-1,t)=1>0$ for $t\ge0$; $E(\tfrac12,t)=-1+\max(t/2,0)>0$ iff $t>2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded grokking ratio]
Let $u_\sigma(t)=-1+\ReLU(t\sigma)$ with $\sigma>0$; its sharp threshold is exactly
$1/\sigma$. Fix $\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}>0$. For every $R$ there is
$\sigma_{\mathrm{test}}>0$ with both thresholds finite and sharp and
\[
\frac{1/\sigma_{\mathrm{test}}}{1/\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}}
= \frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}}{\sigma_{\mathrm{test}}} > R .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\ReLU(t\sigma)\le1$ iff $t\le1/\sigma$. Take
$\sigma_{\mathrm{test}}=\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}/(2(R+1))$ when $R+1>0$, and
$\sigma_{\mathrm{test}}=\sigma_{\mathrm{train}}$ otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Robustness with a sharp displacement]
Let $f(t)\le0$ for $t\le\tau$ and $f(t)\ge\kappa(t-\tau)$ for $t>\tau$, with
$\kappa>0$, and let $|g-f|\le\varepsilon$ uniformly, $\varepsilon\ge0$. Then
$g(t)\le\varepsilon$ for $t\le\tau$ and $g(t)>0$ for $t>\tau+\varepsilon/\kappa$. The
displacement is attained: for $f(t)=\kappa(t-\tau)$ and $g=f-\varepsilon$ the sharp
threshold of $g$ is exactly $\tau+\varepsilon/\kappa$, strictly later than $\tau$
when $\varepsilon>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Before $\tau$: $g\le f+\varepsilon\le\varepsilon$. After $\tau+\varepsilon/\kappa$:
$f(t)\ge\kappa(t-\tau)>\varepsilon$, so $g(t)\ge f(t)-\varepsilon>0$. Sharpness:
$\kappa(t-\tau)-\varepsilon>0$ iff $t>\tau+\varepsilon/\kappa$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
\item[A. Exact delay of a zero-bias network.] Given $c<0$ and per-unit signals
$u_j=a_jg_j\ge0$, return $\tau = |c|/\sum_j u_j$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}); cost
$O(m)$.
\item[B. Sandwich certificate.] Given general $a,b,g,c$, return
$\bigl[|c|/S,\ \min_{j:a_j,g_j>0}(|c|/a_j-b_j)/g_j\bigr]$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:sandwich}); cost $O(m)$. A width-zero interval certifies the exact
answer.
\item[C. Crossing time and criticality test.] Given $\lambda,s,w_0,\theta$, report
$\lambda_c=s/\theta$ and $\mu=s/\lambda-\theta$; if $\mu\le0$ report ``no
transition'', else return $\tau=\lambda^{-1}\log\frac{s/\lambda-w_0}{\mu}$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:cross}, \ref{thm:connection}); cost $O(1)$.
\item[D. Mechanism classifier.] Given measured delays $\tau(\mu_i)$ with
$\mu_i\downarrow0$, fit both $K_1\log(1/\mu)$ and $K_2\mu^{-1/2}$ and compare
residuals, or regress $\log\tau$ on $\log(1/\mu)$ and test whether the slope is near
$1/2$. Theorems~\ref{thm:sharplog} and \ref{thm:sharpsqrt} supply the predicted
constants $1/\lambda_c$ and $\pi$; cost $O(n)$.
\end{description}

\section{Discussion}

In every model treated here the internal state moves smoothly and monotonically
throughout the flat phase: a weight climbing an exponential toward its regularized
optimum, or a state crawling through the remnant of an annihilated equilibrium pair.
The observable is flat because a rectifier clips it, or because a margin has not yet
reached zero. The transition is not a change of internal dynamics but the moment a
monotone quantity crosses a level; the natural experimental object is therefore a
\emph{pre-threshold observable} whose motion is visible during the plateau.

Quantitatively, only two divergence laws appear, they are distinguishable by
exponent, and both constants are computable: $1/\lambda_c$ for relaxation and $\pi$
for the bottleneck. Because the second dominates the first arbitrarily close to
criticality, a system containing both shows a $-1/2$ log--log slope near its critical
point; observing a logarithmic law therefore \emph{excludes} a nearby saddle node in
the reduced dynamics, a falsifiable structural claim about the loss landscape.

The scope should be stated plainly. The ramp $t\mapsto tp$ is a one-parameter probe,
not a full trajectory in weight space; \S\ref{sec:training} supplies an honest
trajectory only for a scalar weight. The sign conditions are what make the margin
monotone, and \S\ref{sec:tropical} shows they are not cosmetic. The connection
Theorem~\ref{thm:connection} is a statement about \emph{parameters}: it identifies
when a transition occurs and makes $\mu$ an explicit function of $\lambda$, but it
does not derive the quadratic vector field from a concrete network's loss. The width
laws assume zero hidden biases for exactness, and independence for the probabilistic
version.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Vector-valued training dynamics.} Derive the ramp from gradient flow in
the full weight space of a finite-width network and prove delayed margin positivity
along the derived trajectory.
\item \textbf{From loss landscape to normal form.} Derive $\mu-x^2$ as the reduced
dynamics of a concrete trained network near a degenerate critical point, making $\mu$
an optimizer parameter by derivation rather than identification.
\item \textbf{A classification theorem for exponents.} Prove or refute that
logarithmic and $\mu^{-1/2}$ are the only delay laws attainable by generic smooth
one-dimensional reductions. A falsification would be a smooth nondegenerate family
with delay $\sim\mu^{-1}$ or $\sim e^{1/\mu}$.
\item \textbf{Width laws beyond independence.} Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:slln} to weakly
dependent or trained (hence correlated) hidden units.
\item \textbf{Counting relapses exactly.} Determine the exact maximal number of
connected components of the failure set of a width-$m$ two-layer ReLU network along a
ray; Theorem~\ref{thm:comb} gives $\ge k+1$ for width $3k$.
\item \textbf{Robustness in the bottleneck regime.} Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:robust}
with Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpsqrt} to show that the constant $\pi$, and not merely the
exponent, persists under uniform perturbations of the vector field.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Delayed generalization, in the models analysed here, is a threshold-crossing
phenomenon with a computable threshold. Its sharpness comes from monotonicity and
survives worst-case aggregation over a test set; its permanence comes from the
convexity of a positively-weighted rectifier layer, and fails exactly when that
positivity fails. Its length obeys one of two laws: threshold relaxation under weight
decay gives $\tau\sim\lambda_c^{-1}\log(1/\mu)$ with a genuine critical decay
$\lambda_c=s/\theta$ beyond which no transition occurs, while a saddle-node
bottleneck gives $T\sim\pi|\mu|^{-1/2}$ with a constant independent of where the
passage is observed. Near criticality the second dominates the first. Two mechanisms,
two exponents, two exact constants --- $1/\lambda_c$ and $\pi$ --- and a measurement
that tells them apart.

